+## 9. Round 3 (2026-10-12) — A-greedy (ngram-mod defaults): results, output identity, B choice
+
+A = `--spec-type ngram-mod` with defaults (n-min 48 / n-max 64 / n-match 24), full production flags
+(mmproj, --kv-unified, q4_0 KV, --parallel 2, --jinja) on :8089, greedy (temp 0, top_k 1, seed 0).
+Data: `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/{off,A}-greedy/` (4 phases each, fresh server per phase).
+
+**mmproj + spec confirmed (rounds 2-3 of the §3 verification list).** No rejection: `A-greedy/notes.txt`
+contains only the cmd + `done`; all 4 phases started, VRAM steady at 9507 MiB (vram.txt). Draft stats
+appear in every phase: `server_single-agent.log` timing lines show per-step draft acceptance
+(e.g. `6 accepted / 192 generated` 0.031, `45 accepted / 128 generated`, `2135 accepted / 2242
+generated` on the long mem0 request). Per-request `draft_n`/`draft_n_accepted` are present on all
+A foreground rows. This is the empirical confirmation §3 asked for: spec is usable with mmproj +
+q4_0 KV + --parallel 2 on text-only traffic.
+
+**Decode tok/s (fg, sum predicted_n / sum predicted_ms):**
+
+| phase | off | A (all) | A (no length) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| single-agent | 55.1 | 86.0 | 54.3 |
+| single-mem | 55.6 | 53.1 | 53.1 |
+| dual-agent | 26.3 | 25.5 | 26.1 |
+| dual-mem | 25.0 | 23.8 | 23.8 |
+
+**A's single-agent 86.0 tok/s is not comparable output.** Two of the 30 requests —
+mem0-r0/0/2 (3121 vs 1020 tok) and mem0-r2/0/2 (4854 vs 3701 tok) — finish `length` in A but
+`tool_calls` in off, i.e. the temp-0 continuation diverged and A ran to the cap (both arms share
+the same recorded predicted_n-derived max_tokens, 3121 and 4854). Excluding the length-finished
+rows, A's single-agent is 54.3 vs 56.2 tok/s — slightly *worse*. Length-finish counts: off 1
+(r1/2/8, 1323 tok); A 3 (that row + the two mem0 rows above; dual-agent A adds r1/2/6 768 tok
+and r1/2/8 1323 tok). Per the advisor rule, both views are reported; the no-length view is the fair
+one.
+
+**Output identity at temperature 0 (off vs A, per phase, out_sha256):** single-agent 3/30,
+single-mem 10/12, dual-agent 0/30, dual-mem 11/12 identical. Consistent with the standing fact
+that even off-greedy outputs differ between single and dual phases (r0/0/0 sha 530c... vs
+4694...; r0/0/8 141 vs 203 tok) — greedy output depends on batch mix, and the spec path changes
+batching. So arm-to-arm identity must be judged per phase, and low match rates are expected; they
+are reported, not hidden.
+
+**Dual-slot verdict (the M1 decision metric): A does not help.** Both dual phases are negative
+(agent −3.0% all / −1.5% no-length; mem −4.8%) and acceptance is low (dual-agent 259/1856 ≈
+14%; dual-mem 69/740 ≈ 9%): the draft/verification overhead outweighs the occasional accepted
+run. A fails the "helps with both slots busy" rule; it is not a deploy candidate. The single-slot
+agent win in the all-rows view is real throughput only on diverged, longer output.
+
+**B choice: `--spec-type ngram-cache`.** From `spec_flags.txt` this is the only ngram variant with
+a different mechanism: a persistent n-gram cache retained across requests, versus ngram-mod's
+per-request lookup. M1's hypothesis is that repeated agent content (file contents, diffs, test
+output) makes drafts often correct — a cross-request cache is the type that most directly exploits
+repetition, including in the dual-phase traffic where ngram-mod's acceptance collapsed. It also
+has no per-variant flags of its own in the help text, so it runs on documented defaults and is a
+clean contrast, not a re-tuning of A. If B also fails dual, the ngram family does not help under
+contention and the stack stays as-is; if it wins dual, it becomes the deploy candidate after the
+prod-sampling runs. B will run as `B greedy --spec-type ngram-cache` in
+`bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/B-greedy/` (same phases, fresh servers). If the server
+refuses ngram-cache, the failure line will be quoted in `B-greedy/notes.txt` and the mmproj
+fallback logic (refusal-only, per §3) handles it.
